        - heading "Viscohypoplastic bentonite (VIBE, Mašín 2026)" [level=2] [ref=e485]
      - generic [ref=e486]:
        - generic [ref=e487]:
          - paragraph [ref=e488]: VIBE combines swelling within clay aggregates, deformation between aggregates and elapsed time to describe the coupled stress and water-retention response of bentonite.
          - paragraph [ref=e489]: The model treats the fine pores within clay aggregates and the larger pores between aggregates separately. Swelling of the fine structure can compress the larger pores even when the specimen volume is fixed. Temperature and suction influence both structures, while a viscous stress contribution allows relaxation during a hold.
          - generic [ref=e490]:
            - heading "What it describes" [level=3] [ref=e491]
            - generic [ref=e492]:
              - term [ref=e493]: Stress relaxation during a hold
              - definition [ref=e494]: With viscosity enabled, stress can change while total strain, suction and temperature are held fixed. The temperature coefficient controls how this time effect grows as the specimen heats.
              - term [ref=e495]: Wetting and drying follow different branches
              - definition [ref=e496]: The hydraulic memory records whether the material follows a main wetting branch, a main drying branch or an intermediate scanning path. The water stored in the fine and larger pores combines into the total saturation.
              - term [ref=e497]: Internal swelling differs from external loading
              - definition [ref=e498]: The model reduces irreversible deformation caused internally by swelling and shrinking of aggregates, while preserving a nonlinear response to an externally imposed load.
          - generic [ref=e499]:
            - heading "Explore relaxation and hydraulic memory" [level=3] [ref=e500]
            - paragraph [ref=e501]: Start with the 1000-second near-boundary relaxation example and compare it with the one-hour hold and the dry-state hold. Then use drying followed by wetting to see the evolving scanning memory. The combined examples add heating or small six-component mechanical loading. Keep the complete starting state when comparing histories.
            - paragraph [ref=e502]: The curves show calculated model responses. No experimental overlay or material calibration is supplied.
          - generic [ref=e503]:
            - heading "Material parameters" [level=3] [ref=e504]
            - paragraph [ref=e505]: The published BCV material values are illustrative defaults. Several source constants are fixed; vf also admits its published rate-independent special case. Editable intervals are bounds for numerical studies, not experimental confidence ranges. Fit compression, retention, swelling and thermal parameters together for a particular material.
            - table [ref=e506]:
              - rowgroup [ref=e507]:
                - row [ref=e508]:
                  - columnheader "Parameter" [ref=e509]
                  - columnheader "Meaning" [ref=e510]
                  - columnheader "Units" [ref=e511]
              - rowgroup [ref=e512]:
                - row [ref=e513]:
