          - generic [ref=e633]:
            - heading "Starting state" [level=3] [ref=e634]
            - paragraph [ref=e635]: Enter all six initial net-stress components, global void ratio, hydraulic scanning memory, suction, absolute temperature and incoming imposed rates. Micro- and macrovoid ratios and saturation are calculated from that complete context. Memory 0 is the wetting branch, 1 the drying branch and intermediate values describe scanning. The default relaxation example uses e = 0.725, memory 0.35, suction 10 MPa and 294 K, with isotropic net pressure about 5.116 MPa chosen near the asymptotic state boundary. This is a numerical starting state, not a reconstructed specimen history.
          - generic [ref=e636]:
            - heading "Scope and limitations" [level=3] [ref=e637]
            - paragraph [ref=e638]: "The selected formulation is the complete published explicit Appendix A law, with physical seconds and prescribed mechanical strain, suction and temperature histories. Stress is net Cauchy stress: total stress minus pore-air pressure times the identity in the compression-positive convention. The interface uses infinitesimal Mandel strain, fixed spatial axes and zero spin, within and on the asymptotic state boundary (OA ≥ 1)."
            - paragraph [ref=e639]: Strictly positive suction and admissible positive micro- and macrostructure pressures, void ratios and viscosity are required. A saturated starting point is excluded by the hydraulic-memory boundary. Examples cover bounded relaxation, heating/drying, hydraulic reversal and small fixed-direction mixed mechanical loading and reversal. Arbitrarily rotating load paths, finite rotations, coupled heat/water transport and a general finite-element tangent are outside this support. Numerical checks do not calibrate a particular bentonite or validate experiments.
          - group [ref=e640]:
